; Pin OpenMP to a single thread for the whole test suite. The test
; workloads are tiny (dozens of rows), so libxgboost spawning one OMP
; thread per core is pure overhead; worse, under opam-repo-ci's
; `dune ... -j <host-cpus>` the test executables run in parallel and
; each spawns host-cpu-count OMP threads, oversubscribing an 80-core
; worker into a ~40-minute thrash. One thread is both faster here and
; keeps libxgboost's FP reductions deterministic across CI workers.
(env
 (_
  (env-vars
   (OMP_NUM_THREADS 1)
   (OMP_THREAD_LIMIT 1))))
